With that same-origin API running and an existing page element stored in output, call `await saveMinutes(45, output);` in an async handler. The first argument is the duration to save; the second is where feedback appears. If the POST succeeds and the following GET returns [{"minutes":45}], output.textContent becomes a formatted JSON array containing that record. The helper returns no data value: its promise resolves to undefined after updating the element. A failed POST instead displays its error and skips the GET.

